message.content:
In C, a **file** is simply a stream of bytes stored on some persistent medium (hard‑disk, SSD, USB stick, network share, etc.).  
The C Standard Library gives you a high‑level, portable way to work with files through the **`FILE`** type and a set of I/O functions defined in `<stdio.h>`.

---

## 1.  The `FILE` type

```c
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *fp;          /* fp is a pointer to a FILE object */
```

* `FILE` is an opaque struct that represents an open file.  
* You never create a `FILE` directly; you obtain one by calling `fopen()` (or related functions).

---

## 2.  Opening a file – `fopen()`

```c
fp = fopen("example.txt", "r");   /* open for reading */
```

| Mode string | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `"r"` | Read text (fails if file doesn’t exist) |
| `"w"` | Write text (creates/truncates file) |
| `"a"` | Append text (creates file if missing) |
| `"rb"` | Read binary |
| `"wb"` | Write binary |
| `"ab"` | Append binary |
| `"r+"` | Read/write text (file must exist) |
| `"w+"` | Read/write text (creates/truncates) |
| `"a+"` | Read/write append (creates if missing) |
| `"r+b"` | Read/write binary |

If `fopen()` fails it returns `NULL`; check with `errno` or `perror()`.

---

## 3.  Closing a file – `fclose()`

```c
if (fclose(fp) != 0) {
    perror("Error closing file");
}
```

Always close a file when you’re done to flush buffers and release resources.

---

## 4.  Reading and writing

| Function | Purpose | Example |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `fgetc(fp)` | Read one character | `int ch = fgetc(fp);` |
| `fgets(buf, n, fp)` | Read a line (up to `n-1` chars) | `char buf[256]; fgets(buf, sizeof buf, fp);` |
| `fread(ptr, size, nmemb, fp)` | Read binary data | `size_t n = fread(buf, 1, 100, fp);` |
| `fprintf(fp, format, …)` | Write formatted text | `fprintf(fp, "Score: %d\n", score);` |
| `fwrite(ptr, size, nmemb, fp)` | Write binary data | `fwrite(buf, 1, 100, fp);` |
| `fscanf(fp, format, …)` | Read formatted input | `int age; fscanf(fp, "%d", &age);` |

All these functions return a value that indicates success or failure (`EOF` for `fgetc`, `fgets`, `fscanf`; number of items read for `fread`, `fwrite`; non‑zero for `fprintf`, `fscanf` on error).

---

## 5.  Seeking and telling

```c
fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END);   /* move to end of file */
long size = ftell(fp);     /* get current position (file size) */
rewind(fp);                /* equivalent to fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_SET) */
```

Useful for random access or determining file size.

---

## 6.  Error handling

```c
if (feof(fp)) { /* end‑of‑file reached */ }
if (ferror(fp)) { /* read/write error */ }
clearerr(fp); /* reset error flags */
```

---

## 7.  Low‑level I/O (POSIX)

For more control (especially on Unix/Linux), you can use file descriptors:

```c
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int fd = open("example.bin", O_RDONLY);
ssize_t n = read(fd, buf, 100);
close(fd);
```

These functions are not portable to Windows without a POSIX layer.

---

## 8.  Example program

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    FILE *fp = fopen("data.txt", "w+");
    if (!fp) {
        perror("fopen");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    /* Write some data */
    fprintf(fp, "Hello, world!\n");
    fprintf(fp, "Number: %d\n", 42);

    /* Seek back to beginning */
    rewind(fp);

    /* Read and print */
    char line[128];
    while (fgets(line, sizeof line, fp)) {
        printf("%s", line);
    }

    /* Clean up */
    if (fclose(fp) != 0) {
        perror("fclose");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Compile with `gcc -Wall -Wextra -o file_demo file_demo.c`.

---

### TL;DR

* A file in C is represented by a `FILE *` obtained via `fopen()`.  
* Use the standard I/O functions (`fread`, `fwrite`, `fprintf`, `fscanf`, etc.) to read/write.  
* Always close the file with `fclose()`.  
* For binary or random‑access needs, use `fseek`/`ftell`.  
* On POSIX systems, you can also use low‑level `open`, `read`, `write`, `close`.

That’s the core of file handling in C.
